      }
     ],
     "then": {
      "tex": "\\mk{c}{\\kvc} = \\mk{m}{\\min_{\\kq}\\frac{\\komegaq}{\\kq}}",
      "uses": [0],
      "why": "The object can create an excitation once $v$ exceeds $\\omega_q/q$ for some $q$."
     },
     "steps": [
      {
       "tex": "\\mk{c}{\\kvc} = \\mk{m}{\\min_{\\kq}\\frac{\\sqrt{\\kepsq^2 + 2\\knzero\\kUzero\\kepsq}}{\\hbar\\kq}}",
       "uses": [
        1
       ]
      },
      {
       "tex": "\\mk{c}{\\kvc} = \\mk{m}{\\min_{\\kq}\\sqrt{\\frac{\\hbar^2\\kq^2}{4\\km^2} + \\frac{\\knzero\\kUzero}{\\km}}}",
       "uses": [
        2
       ]
      },
      {
       "tex": "\\mk{c}{\\kvc} = \\mk{m}{\\sqrt{\\frac{\\knzero\\kUzero}{\\km}}}",
       "uses": [],
       "cap": "The root grows with $q$, so its least value is reached as $q \\to 0$."
      },
      {
       "tex": "\\mk{c}{\\kvc} = \\mk{m}{\\kssound}",
       "uses": [
        3
       ]
      }
     ],
     "num": "(11.159)",
     "note": "The book prints $\\Delta\\keps_\\mathrm{object} = \\komegaq$ in (11.158) and $\\kvmag > \\kvc = \\kepsq/\\hbar\\kq$ in (11.159), the free-particle energy over the momentum. The energy given to the excitation is $\\hbar\\komegaq$, so the condition is $\\kvmag \\geq \\komegaq/\\kq$ for some $\\kq$, and the critical velocity is the least $\\komegaq/\\kq$, the speed of sound $\\kssound$."
    }
